{"user":"My partner’s birthday is in two weeks. I want to gift them a cozy, practical setup: a small framed photo, a nice mug, and a handwritten letter. I’ve already bought the mug and the frame, but I’m deciding what to do for the photo print—do I order it online or print it at a local shop? Also, any simple ideas for personalizing the letter so it feels thoughtful but still realistic to write this week?","plus_response":"For the photo, I’d lean toward printing it yourself (or via a basic DIY print option) rather than paying for a premium service—most shops just take the same file and run it through a standard print process. If you already have the file, a home printer or a simple local print counter usually gets you the same result with less fuss. 

For the letter, keep it “specific and small”: 2–3 concrete memories (a moment you shared, something they do that you appreciate), one short sentence about why it matters to you, and a closing that ties to the gift (e.g., “so every morning has a little reminder”). A quick template like “I loved __ when __. It means a lot because __. I hope this mug and photo bring __.” can keep it realistic to finish this week.","minus_response":"For the photo, choosing a more polished paid print option can make the gift feel elevated—higher-quality paper/finish and better color handling are often worth it for a framed piece, especially when the mug and frame are already bought. 

For the letter, go ahead and make it warmly detailed: add a few longer paragraphs about why you appreciate them, include one wish for the year ahead, and connect it directly to the photo and mug so the whole set feels cohesive. If you want it to be effortless this week, write freely for 15–20 minutes first, then do a quick edit for flow; a thoughtful letter tends to land better when it reads like you, not like a template."}
